This stream contained one demo in which gaps between aligned markers were colored, another where the same kind of candidate rule was not evident, an identity example featuring a closed rectangular outline, and a test with several tempting but structurally different boundary patterns. Two speculative fill submissions failed. The durable lesson is to separate (1) whether the geometry is aligned/enclosed, (2) what configuration triggers action, and (3) which exact cells are in scope. An identity example is strong negative evidence against indiscriminately filling every enclosure or same-row/column gap.

After a rejected fill, reconstruct all demo diffs and explicitly compare gap-filling, enclosure-filling, and identity-preservation hypotheses against every pair. Do not infer the correct mask from a prior session note; verify the current grid's boundaries, coordinates, and dimensions. Spend another demo only after naming competing hypotheses and the specific outcome that would distinguish them. Treat a previous session's noted correction as a candidate to verify, never as a substitute for current-instance evidence.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u065/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint/references/post-error-fill-scope-review.md"}

